  "headline_findings": [
    "Day 15: five selectors (MI, RF, BPSO, QPSO, QIGPSO) benchmarked over 5 seeds with 95% CIs, plus stability and redundancy.",
    "Day 17-18: NEW selector->zero-day-AUROC experiment run on all three datasets.",
    "BoT-IoT and CIC-IoT2023: every selector preserves zero-day separability (AUROC 0.97-0.98); feature selection does NOT harm novelty geometry there.",
    "UNSW-NB15: zero-day AUROC is ~0.29-0.32, i.e. BELOW CHANCE, for EVERY selector including no-selection. The held-out families (Backdoor/Analysis) sit INSIDE the known-traffic manifold.",
    "This independently reproduces Team A's Day-13 finding that UNSW zero-day families are below chance for every unsupervised head -- so it is a property of the DATASET, not of our feature selection.",
    "Implication for Team B: conformal zero-day rejection cannot work on UNSW with unsupervised scores on these 17 features, regardless of which subset is used.",
    "Selection choice barely moves zero-day AUROC (<0.01 spread on BoT/CIC) -- so subsets can be chosen on accuracy/qubit cost without harming novelty detection."
